WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.789
You have a million log lines and need to know whether any device ID repeats. Comparing every pair would take all day. A hash table answers in one pass.

00:00:12.989 --> 00:00:13.918
Pause and think.

00:00:14.318 --> 00:00:17.627
What happens to lookups if every key lands in the same bucket?

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:25.341
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.864
This lesson explains hash tables, the most useful data structure in coding interviews.

00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:42.401
New word: hash table. A structure that stores values by key using a hash function for near-instant lookup. Picture it like this: Coat-check hooks numbered by your ticket.

00:00:44.951 --> 00:00:46.739
Think of a coat check at a theater.

00:00:47.189 --> 00:00:49.871
You hand over your coat and get a ticket number.

00:00:50.321 --> 00:00:55.603
Later you show the ticket, and the attendant walks straight to that hook. No searching through every coat.

00:00:56.053 --> 00:01:01.069
A hash table does the same thing: a hash function turns your key into a hook number.

00:01:01.184 --> 00:01:11.041
New word: hash function. A function that turns any key into a fixed-size number. Picture it like this: A machine that turns a name into a locker number.

00:01:13.591 --> 00:01:16.737
Here we have eight buckets, numbered zero to seven.

00:01:17.187 --> 00:01:23.282
To store the key seventeen, we compute seventeen mod eight, which is one. It goes in bucket one.

00:01:23.732 --> 00:01:26.879
The key twenty five also gives one. That is a collision.

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:30.069
We simply chain it in a short list in the same bucket.

00:01:30.519 --> 00:01:33.967
Lookups compute the bucket, then scan that tiny list.

00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:39.392
On average, insert, lookup, and delete are all O of one.

00:01:39.842 --> 00:01:43.824
The worst case is O of n, if every key lands in the same bucket.

00:01:44.274 --> 00:01:51.159
To keep chains short, the table grows and rehashes when it gets too full. That threshold is called the load factor.

00:01:51.609 --> 00:01:57.599
In C plus plus this is unordered map. In Java it is HashMap. In C sharp it is Dictionary.

00:01:57.657 --> 00:02:04.739
New word: HashMap. Java's hash table class. Picture it like this: Coat-check hooks numbered by ticket.

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:09.751
Here is how Two Sum uses a hash table.

00:02:10.201 --> 00:02:17.120
We walk the array once. For each number we ask the table: have I already seen the partner, target minus me?

00:02:17.272 --> 00:02:25.712
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:02:27.322 --> 00:02:31.119
For two, we need seven. Not there yet, so we store two.

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:37.420
For seven, we need two. The table says yes, at index zero. Done in one pass.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.893
A few systems notes for staff level interviews.

00:02:42.343 --> 00:02:48.415
Hash tables have poor locality when chains are pointers, so open addressing is often faster.

00:02:48.539 --> 00:02:56.898
New word: pointer. A variable holding the memory address of something else. Picture it like this: A note saying "the book is on shelf 4".

00:02:58.548 --> 00:03:01.857
Iteration order is not guaranteed, so never depend on it.

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:07.323
Under concurrency, shard the table or use a concurrent map instead of one big lock.

00:03:08.673 --> 00:03:14.675
In the interview, say out loud why a hash map fits: you need fast membership or counting.

00:03:15.125 --> 00:03:18.074
Mention the trade: you spend memory to buy time.

00:03:18.524 --> 00:03:23.586
And remember the classic bug in Two Sum: check for the partner before inserting yourself.

00:03:24.936 --> 00:03:28.883
Whenever you hear "have I seen this before?", reach for a hash table.

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:30.095
Back to the question.

00:03:30.495 --> 00:03:33.804
What happens to lookups if every key lands in the same bucket?

00:03:34.204 --> 00:03:38.848
They slow to O of n, because the bucket becomes one long list to search.

00:03:40.048 --> 00:03:40.918
Quick recap.

00:03:41.318 --> 00:03:43.675
A hash turns a key into a bucket number.

00:03:44.075 --> 00:03:45.724
Average lookup is O of 1.

00:03:46.124 --> 00:03:49.142
Use them for counting, seen-before and grouping.
